In this lesson, we'll look at complementary events. An event is just something that could happen, like rolling a six or drawing a red card. The complement of that event is everything that is not it. So if your event is rolling a six, the complement is rolling anything that isn't a six. Together, the event and its complement cover every possible outcome, which means their probabilities always add up to one. That's a really handy trick. If it's easier to work out the chance of the complement, you can just find that, then subtract from one to get the probability you actually want. For example, finding the chance of at least one head in three coin tosses sounds tricky, but the complement is no heads at all, which is much simpler to count. One quick warning though. Complementary and mutually exclusive are not the same thing. Complementary events cover every outcome, while mutually exclusive just means they can't happen together. Rolling a one and rolling a two are mutually exclusive, but they're not complementary, because you could also roll a three, four, five, or six. Now let's see it in action.